 must choose a tile to **play** from the valid list.\n\nValid actions are all \u201cPlay X\u201d \u2014 meaning I must discard one tile.\n\nBest candidates to discard:\n- **W6**: No sequence possible (W5-W7 are out), not part of any future plan.\n- **W9**: Terminal, hard to use unless in terminal chow or honor/terminal hand.\n- **T3**: Discarded by others; low utility in my hand.\n- **B6**: Very isolated; no nearby dots except B5/B7 which aren\u2019t here.\n- **J2**: Honor, singleton, not forming a pung.\n\nBut note: I already have a pung of F1 (East Wind), and East is my seat wind \u2192 qualifies for **Seat Wind (2 points)** pattern. If I can get a dragon pung, I can reach 8+ points.\n\nCurrently, I have one J2 \u2014 if I keep it, maybe draw more. But unlikely.\n\nAlternatively, focus on **All Chows (2 points)** + **Concealed Hand** (not anymore, since I Peng\u2019d). But Peng breaks full concealment \u2192 so **Fully Concealed Hand** (4 pts) is gone. However, **Concealed Hand** (2 pts) requires all melds concealed and win by stealing \u2014 not applicable since I have an exposed Peng.\n\nSo my hand is **exposed**, thus patterns like Fully Concealed Hand are invalid. But **Melded Hand** (6 pts) is possible \u2014 if every meld is completed by stealing, including the pair. But I already drew and formed W1-W2-W3 myself \u2014 those are concealed. So not fully melded.\n\nThus, aiming for **All Chows (2 pts)** is viable \u2014 I have one pung (F1), so I need to convert that idea\u2026 wait: I must have four chows and a pair for All Chows. But I have a pung of winds \u2014 that\u2019s not a chow. So unless I break it (can't), **All Chows** is impossible.\n\nSimilarly, **Half Flush** (honor + one suit): possible if I go for W or B. I have many W tiles: W1, W2, W3, W6, W9 \u2014 five distinct, plus F1 pung. Could aim for Half Flush in Characters.\n\nBut better: consider **Outside Hand (4 pts)**: every meld contains terminals or honors. My F1 pung qualifies. If I make chows involving W1-W2-W3 (contains W1, terminal), and perhaps pair with B1 (also terminal), then possibly qualify.\n\nEven better: **One Voided Suit (1 pt)** \u2014 currently I have W, T, B \u2014 all three suits present. If I discard T3, I eliminate Bamboo? No, I still have B1, B2, B6. Discarding T3 removes the last T, though \u2014 wait: do I have other Bamboos?\n\nHand: W1, W2, W3, W6, W9, T3, B1, B1, B2, B6, J2 \u2192 so yes, T3 is my only Bamboo. If I discard T3, then I have only W and B and honors \u2014 still two suited suits. To achieve **One Voided Suit**, I need only *one* suited suit. Currently I have both W and B.\n\nSo to maximize chance of One Voided Suit, I should eliminate either W or B. But I have more Ws (5) than Bs (4), so perhaps keep W, discard B-tiles.\n\nBut B1 pair is good \u2014 better than single Ws.\n\nAlternative strategy: build toward **Seven Pairs (24 pts)**? I only have one pair (B1,B1). Need six more pairs \u2014 unlikely.\n\nOr **Chicken Hand (8 pts)** \u2014 i.e., a random legal hand worth exactly 8 points with minimal patterns. Possible if I can get:\n\n- Seat Wind (F1 pung) = 2 pts\n- Dragon Pung? Not yet.\n- Maybe Pung of Terminals or Honors (F1) = 1 pt\n- If I form a pair of honors or terminals, another 1 pt\n\nBut I need at least 8.\n\nWait: Chicken Hand itself is worth 8 points \u2014 defined as a hand with zero fans otherwise, but allowed under MCR as a minimum qualifying hand. Yes: **Chicken Hand** is a valid 8-point pattern when no other patterns apply.\n\nSo if my hand has no scoring patterns at all, but is legal, I can still win with **Chicken Hand (8 pts)**.\n\nTherefore, even a loosely formed hand can qualify.\n\nMy goal is to improve towards completion.\n\nBack to discards:\n\nBest candidates: **W6**, **W9**, **B6**, **T3**\n\nAmong these:\n- **T3**: I have only one, and it's in a suit where I have no other support. Across player has T6-T7-T8, previous has T3-T4-T5 \u2014 so T3 was used in a chow already \u2192 likely safe to discard.\n- But discarding **T3** removes the only Bamboo, simplifying hand.\n- However, I have W1-W2-W3 \u2014 a complete chow \u2014 and W6, W9 are strays.\n\nBetter to keep the chow and dump outliers.\n\nSo best discards: **W6** or **W9**\n\nW6: middle tile, could help someone with 5-6-7 or 4-5-6. But W5-W7 are already used (next player has W5-W6-W7 meld) \u2192 so W6 is part of their meld \u2192 they already have it \u2192 safe to discard? They already completed the chow, so probably don\u2019t need another W6. But could be going for kong? Unlikely.\n\nStill, W6 is safer than W9? W9 is terminal, always dangerous.\n\nSimilarly, B6 is mid-dot, but B5, B7 not seen much \u2014 could be okay.\n\nBut I have **two B1s** \u2014 a pair! That could be my eye.\n\nSo ideal: finish hand with [F1,F1,F1], [W1,W2,W3], [B1,B1], and two more chows.\n\nNeed to form chows in W or B.\n\nAvailable: W6, W9 \u2014 hard to connect.\n\nB2 and B6 \u2014 B1-B2 could start 1-2-3, but missing B3.\n\nIf I keep B1,B1,B2, and draw B3, I can form [B1,B2,B3] and keep B1 as pair? No \u2014 conflict.\n\nBetter: use B1 pair as eyes, then use W1-W2-W3 as one chow, need three more chows.\n\nOther tiles: W6, W9, B6 \u2014 all orphans.\n\nSo best to discard **W6** or **W9**.\n\nBetween them, W6 is more central, potentially useful to others, but W9 is terminal \u2014 higher risk.\n\nBut W9: only one way to use \u2014 7-8-9. Next player has W5-W6-W7 \u2014 close, but not overlapping. Previous player had W3-W4-W5 \u2014 not near. So W7-W8-W9 possible? But W8 not seen.\n\nUnshown W9: 3 left \u2014 I have one, so 3 remain. Likely safe.\n\nBut statistically, middle tiles are claimed more often than terminals.\n\nActually, in practice, terminals are often safer late, but here game is mid-stage.\n\nAnother idea: **Self Drawn** (1 pt) if I win by self-draw, plus **Seat Wind** (2 pts), plus **Pung of Honors** (1 pt for F1), plus maybe **No Honors**? No \u2014 I have honors.\n\nCan I get **All Simples**? No \u2014 I have terminals and honors.\n\nBack to decision.\n\nTop options:\n- **Play W6**: frees up space, keeps potential for W9 to be used later (e.g., if I draw W7,W8)\n- **Play W9**: similar\n- **Play B6**: most useless Dot, not connecting to anything\n- **Play T3**: eliminates Bamboo suit entirely\n\nBut I have **only one T tile**, so discarding T3 gives me a clean two-suited hand: W and B, plus honors.\n\nBut I want to minimize suits eventually.\n\nHowever, **One Voided Suit (1 pt)** requires exactly two suits \u2192 I currently have three (W, T, B) \u2192 if I discard T3, I will have only W and B \u2192 qualifies for 1 pt.\n\nYes!\n\nSo **discarding T3** achieves **One Voided Suit (1 pt)**.\n\nThen, combined with:\n- **Pung of Seat Wind (F1)** \u2192 2 pts\n- **Pung of Honors (F1)** \u2192 1 pt\n- Possibly **Single Wait** or other minor if I win appropriately\n\nTotal: at least 4 pts \u2014 still need 4 more.\n\nBut **Chicken Hand (8 pts)** covers everything \u2014 so as long as I can complete a legal hand, I can rely on Chicken Hand.\n\nTherefore, maximizing flexibility is key.\n\nDiscarding **T3** cleans up the hand and secures **One Voided Suit (1 pt)**.\n\nOther discards don't give that benefit.\n\nAlso, T3 is not useful to me \u2014 I have no other T except this one.\n\nWhereas keeping W6 or W9 might help form a terminal chow later? Unlikely.\n\nSo **Play T3** is optimal.\n\nIt improves hand structure, reduces suit count, and positions for a scoring pattern.\n\nFinal choice: **Play T3**\n\nAnswer: Play T3"}
